    "summary": "Tight first set, soaring Space, mixed second‑set flow",
    "blurb": "The show opens with a lively first set featuring a laughing Jerry on Mississippi Half-Step Uptown Toodeloo and tight renditions of Candyman and China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider. Highlights include a scorching All Along The Watchtower and an expansive Space that showcases the drums as a giant chrome motor. The second set starts strong but loses focus toward the end, with sloppy moments on Louie Louie and Sugar Magnolia.",
    "review": "April 22, 1988 at Irvine Meadows captures the Dead fresh off their East‑Coast run, firing up a first set that feels both tight and high‑energy. Jerry is in fine form – his laugh at the start of Mississippi Half-Step Uptown Toodeloo sets a relaxed tone, and his shredding solos on Watchtower and China Cat > Rider draw cheers throughout. Brent adds synth‑drummed textures during Space, while Mickey and Billy drive the band like a “giant chrome motor,” especially in the Drums > Space segment.\nThe second set begins with the classic China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider pairing and a powerful Estimated Prophet into He’s Gone, but momentum slips as the night wears on. Louie Louie gets a fiery cover, yet later jams feel unfocused, and Sugar Magnolia ends on a sloppy note. The encore closes with a heartfelt Black Muddy River that lifts the show back up. Overall, the performance shines in its early moments and standout songs, though the latter half shows signs of fatigue.",
